WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.599 --> 00:00:02.550
A rude Hollywood screenwriter.

00:00:02.550 --> 00:00:05.100
A man undone by his high school sweetheart.

00:00:05.100 --> 00:00:07.770
A small role on The Big Bang Theory?!

00:00:07.770 --> 00:00:12.490
Lance Barber has played a ton of roles beyond
"Young Sheldon" — keep watching to learn

00:00:12.490 --> 00:00:13.490
more.

00:00:13.490 --> 00:00:17.450
One of the most prominent running gags in
"The Big Bang Theory" involved Sheldon telling

00:00:17.450 --> 00:00:19.810
stories about his childhood in Texas.

00:00:19.810 --> 00:00:23.840
Sheldon's stories portrayed a group of dysfunctional
countrified southerners.

00:00:23.840 --> 00:00:27.760
They couldn't be more different than the timid
and analytical Sheldon.

00:00:27.760 --> 00:00:32.259
However, fans have now gotten a firsthand
perspective of Sheldon's family with the spin-off

00:00:32.259 --> 00:00:34.300
series "Young Sheldon."

00:00:34.300 --> 00:00:38.370
Sheldon sometimes described his father as
a crazed drunk who used his wife's favorite

00:00:38.370 --> 00:00:39.969
cookware for shooting practice.

00:00:39.969 --> 00:00:44.460
However, "Young Sheldon" portrays him as a
simple, blue-collar football coach who does

00:00:44.460 --> 00:00:47.249
his best for his family even if he makes mistakes.

00:00:47.249 --> 00:00:52.929
The two versions of George Cooper Sr. don't
always line up, but neither one is strictly

00:00:52.929 --> 00:00:53.929
bad.

00:00:53.929 --> 00:00:57.499
Actor Lance Barber makes the most out of playing
George on "Young Sheldon."

00:00:57.499 --> 00:01:00.959
However, George Cooper is far from Barber's
only role.

00:01:00.959 --> 00:01:05.140
Throughout his career, he has played a number
of other characters that may make George Cooper

00:01:05.140 --> 00:01:07.920
Sr. from "Young Sheldon" look familiar.

00:01:07.920 --> 00:01:11.040
Lance Barber has no delusions about his level
of fame.

00:01:11.040 --> 00:01:15.619
Barber has been a career actor for over 20
years but has rarely held the spotlight.

00:01:15.619 --> 00:01:19.280
Instead, Barber has made his living playing
a series of smaller roles.

00:01:19.280 --> 00:01:22.719
His earliest role was as a man in a coma on
"ER."

00:01:22.719 --> 00:01:27.520
Later roles included parts in "Yes, Dear",
"What I Like About You," and "Come to Papa."

00:01:27.520 --> 00:01:30.509
In 2014, Barber told the "Battle Creek Enquirer":

00:01:30.509 --> 00:01:34.360
"I was a character guy, I was heavy, I was
likable, I guess.

00:01:34.360 --> 00:01:37.909
So I had the opportunity to play best friend
guy to the lead guy."

00:01:37.909 --> 00:01:41.940
Despite his lack of mainstream notoriety,
he still loves being an actor:

00:01:41.940 --> 00:01:43.580
"I feel very lucky.

00:01:43.580 --> 00:01:46.960
As an actor we all want more work, and more
quality work.

00:01:46.960 --> 00:01:48.409
But I have zero complaints."

00:01:48.409 --> 00:01:52.409
Being Sheldon's dad isn't Barber's only recognizable
performance.

00:01:52.409 --> 00:01:57.219
Years before "The Big Bang Theory" debuted,
Barber landed a recurring spot in one of HBO's

00:01:57.219 --> 00:01:59.649
most underrated series, "The Comeback".

00:01:59.649 --> 00:02:03.649
The show was about an aging sitcom actress
named Valerie Cherish.

00:02:03.649 --> 00:02:07.369
Valerie allows a film crew to document her
return to show business.

00:02:07.369 --> 00:02:11.280
Like "The Office" and "Arrested Development,"
"The Comeback" reveled in creating awkward

00:02:11.280 --> 00:02:13.220
moments between its characters.

00:02:13.220 --> 00:02:17.440
On the show, Lance Barber played the role
of Paulie G., the showrunner of Valerie's

00:02:17.440 --> 00:02:20.080
latest sitcom gig, "Room and Bored."

00:02:20.080 --> 00:02:24.690
The two maintain an ongoing feud throughout
"The Comeback" and remain enemies even after

00:02:24.690 --> 00:02:26.940
the fictional sitcom is canceled.

00:02:26.940 --> 00:02:31.980
Unfortunately, unlike "The Office," it never
reached a high degree of mainstream popularity.

00:02:31.980 --> 00:02:34.150
The show was canceled after just one season.

00:02:34.150 --> 00:02:37.290
That is, until "The Comeback" made a comeback.

00:02:37.290 --> 00:02:40.870
Over the years, the series had garnered a
cult following on the Internet.

00:02:40.870 --> 00:02:45.540
In 2014, the show was revived for a second
season, reintroducing the main characters

00:02:45.540 --> 00:02:47.100
nine years later.

00:02:47.100 --> 00:02:51.330
In an interview with Bustle, Barber called
the show's return "a dream come true."

00:02:51.330 --> 00:02:53.400
This time, Paulie G. is different.

00:02:53.400 --> 00:02:57.980
When we meet him in 2014, he's gotten clean
following his addiction to heroin during the

00:02:57.980 --> 00:02:59.530
events of Season 1.

00:02:59.530 --> 00:03:03.860
He seems much more willing to work with Valerie
instead of against her, though it isn't long

00:03:03.860 --> 00:03:06.510
before new tensions arise in their relationship.

00:03:06.510 --> 00:03:10.760
Aside from "The Comeback," the latter half
of the 2000s saw primarily bit roles for Lance

00:03:10.760 --> 00:03:11.760
Barber.

00:03:11.760 --> 00:03:15.820
He had one-episode appearances in a variety
of shows, including "Joey," "The Mentalist,"

00:03:15.820 --> 00:03:16.840
"Monk," and "CSI: Miami".

00:03:16.840 --> 00:03:23.870
In the 2010s, he showed up in even more series,
like "Grey's Anatomy," "Key and Peele," "How

00:03:23.870 --> 00:03:28.680
I Met Your Mother," "Bob's Burgers," "Brooklyn
Nine-Nine," and "Black-ish", and "Faking It".

00:03:28.680 --> 00:03:33.411
His most recognizable role during this time,
however, was as Bill Ponderosa in "It's Always

00:03:33.411 --> 00:03:34.910
Sunny in Philadelphia."

00:03:34.910 --> 00:03:39.040
Like most characters in the series, Bill Ponderosa
is a certifiable scumbag:

00:03:39.040 --> 00:03:40.950
"Diddly Dee - miss me?

00:03:40.950 --> 00:03:42.380
Don't answer that.

00:03:42.380 --> 00:03:43.380
Alright."

00:03:43.380 --> 00:03:47.070
Although Dee once had a crush on him in high
school, he's a hedonistic narcissist in the

00:03:47.070 --> 00:03:48.070
present day.

00:03:48.070 --> 00:03:52.070
He cheats on his wife, abuses his kids, and
is addicted to practically everything under

00:03:52.070 --> 00:03:53.070
the sun.

00:03:53.070 --> 00:03:57.400
He also briefly becomes Dennis' brother-in-law
after Dennis marries and then divorces his

00:03:57.400 --> 00:03:58.430
sister, Maureen.

00:03:58.430 --> 00:04:03.500
Bill was last seen in Season 13, where he's
invited by Frank to attend the Super Bowl.

00:04:03.500 --> 00:04:07.370
So, why would Barber look so familiar to "Big
Bang Theory" fans?

00:04:07.370 --> 00:04:11.460
Long before "Young Sheldon" was announced,
Lance Barber actually had a one-time appearance

00:04:11.460 --> 00:04:14.170
on "The Big Bang Theory" as a different character.

00:04:14.170 --> 00:04:17.961
Whenever you see George Cooper Sr. being a
well-meaning dad on "Young Sheldon," you're

00:04:17.961 --> 00:04:22.190
also looking at Leonard's high school bully,
Jimmy Speckerman.

00:04:22.190 --> 00:04:26.260
Speckerman shows up in 2011's "The Speckerman
Recurrence," which focuses on Speckerman's

00:04:26.260 --> 00:04:28.880
failed attempts to make amends with Leonard:

00:04:28.880 --> 00:04:33.400
"Holy crap, man, it's good to see you!"

00:04:33.400 --> 00:04:35.750
However, Speckerman doesn't actually have
good intentions.

00:04:35.750 --> 00:04:40.290
He only apologizes to Leonard while drunk,
and then denies his actions the next day.

00:04:40.290 --> 00:04:43.000
Sheldon helps his friend Leonard stand up
to the bully.

00:04:43.000 --> 00:04:46.930
He never seems to notice that Speckerman looks
just like his father.

